        {
          "field": "adaptation",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "One word for the organization and for its officers together; for one officer English needs police officer. Many languages have a single countable word for an officer and a different word for the force; keep the two apart.",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "adaptation",
          "reason": "Adaptation notes must be language-neutral and must not name languages."
        },
        {
          "field": "adaptation",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "Police is plural in grammar though singular in form: the police are, not the police is. No article a: a police officer, not a police. It has no plural. Learners of many backgrounds make both mistakes.",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "adaptation",
          "reason": "Adaptation notes must be language-neutral and must not refer to learners by background."
        },
        {
          "field": "adaptation",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "The police in English-speaking countries are organized locally, by city or region, so a police department or a police force is a local body. The emergency telephone number differs from country to country. Cop is the common informal word for an officer.",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "adaptation",
          "reason": "The cultural generalization about police organization in all English-speaking countries is too broad and misleading."
        },
        {
          "field": "adaptation",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "In some languages a look-alike word means an insurance document. English police is only the law-keeping organization; an insurance document is a policy.",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "adaptation",
          "reason": "Adaptation notes must be language-neutral and must not name languages."
        },
        {
          "field": "adaptation",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "Stress on the second syllable, with a weak first vowel and a long ee: puh-LEESS. Learners often stress the first syllable; keep the stress on the second. The final sound is s, not z.",
          "severity": "minor",
          "family": "pronunciation",
          "reason": "The respelling \"puh-LEESS\" misleadingly suggests an overly long final vowel for American English."
        },
        {
